        <div style="font-size: 11px; color: #c7922c; margin-bottom: 14px;">&#127919; BY THE END OF THIS LESSON YOU WILL BE ABLE TO&hellip;</div>
        <div style="display: flex; align-items: flex-start; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px;">
            <div style="min-width: 26px; height: 26px; border-radius: 50%; background: #C7922C; color: #fff; font-size: 13px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0;">1</div>
            <div style="font-size: 18px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65;"><strong>I will be able to</strong> describe what Hood&rsquo;s Texas <a href="#glossary" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="A large group of soldiers made up of several regiments.">brigade</a> did in the war in Virginia and Maryland.</div>
        </div>
        <div style="display: flex; align-items: flex-start; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px;">
            <div style="min-width: 26px; height: 26px; border-radius: 50%; background: #C7922C; color: #fff; font-size: 13px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0;">2</div>
            <div style="font-size: 18px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65;"><strong>I will be able to</strong> explain what the Trans-Mississippi <a href="#glossary" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="A large region where part of a war is fought.">theater</a> was and where Texans fought in it.</div>
        </div>
        <div style="display: flex; align-items: flex-start; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px;">
            <div style="min-width: 26px; height: 26px; border-radius: 50%; background: #C7922C; color: #fff; font-size: 13px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; flex-shrink: 0;">3</div>
            <div style="font-size: 18px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65;"><strong>I will be able to</strong> describe how the war reached Texas through the cotton trade, the coast, and the border.</div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <div style="background: #FFFFFF; border: 1px solid #D0D9E8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 20px 24px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <div style="font-size: 11px; color: #c7922c; margin-bottom: 14px;">&#128203; WHAT YOU NEED FOR THIS LESSON</div>
        <div style="display: flex; align-items: flex-start; gap: 10px; margin-bottom: 8px; border-left: 3px solid #D0D9E8; padding-left: 14px;"><span style="color: #c7922c; font-size: 18px; flex-shrink: 0;">&#9654;</span>
            <div style="font-size: 18px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.6;">This page.</div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <div style="background: #FFFFFF; border-top: 3px solid #C7922C; border: 1px solid #D0D9E8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 20px 24px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <div style="font-size: 11px; color: #c7922c; margin-bottom: 14px;">&#128218; PART 1 &middot; THE WAR IN THE EAST</div>
<h3 style="font-size: 20px; color: #0e1c42; margin: 18px 0 8px 0;">The Texas Brigade</h3><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">In 1861, Texans rushed to join the Confederate army. Three Texas <a href="#glossary" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="A unit of several hundred to a thousand soldiers.">regiment</a>s went to Virginia. There they became part of the Army of Northern Virginia, led by General Robert E. Lee. They were soon known as Hood&rsquo;s Texas <a href="#glossary" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="A large group of soldiers made up of several regiments.">brigade</a>, after John Bell Hood, an officer who had served in Texas before the war.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">Lee used the Texans for hard jobs. At Gaines&rsquo;s Mill, in June 1862, they led a charge that broke the Union line. At Second Manassas that summer, they fought again. At Antietam in Maryland, in September 1862, the 1st Texas lost more than 80 of every 100 men in a single day. At Gettysburg in July 1863, the Texans attacked on the second day of the battle. Hood himself was badly wounded there.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">In May 1864, in a tangle of woods called the Wilderness, Lee rode forward to lead the Texans into a charge himself. They shouted, &ldquo;Lee to the rear!&rdquo; They refused to attack until he went back. It shows how much the soldiers trusted him.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">Hood did not stay with the brigade. He was wounded again at Chickamauga in Georgia in September 1863 and lost a leg. In 1864 he took command of an entire army, the Army of Tennessee, and lost most of it in failed attacks at Atlanta, Franklin, and Nashville. The same daring that made him a fine brigade leader was a danger in command of a whole army.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">These men fought bravely. They also fought for a Confederacy whose leaders had said that protecting slavery was a reason for leaving the Union. A fair account holds both facts.</p>
    </div>
    <div style="background: #FFFFFF; border-top: 3px solid #C7922C; border: 1px solid #D0D9E8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 20px 24px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <div style="font-size: 11px; color: #c7922c; margin-bottom: 14px;">&#128218; PART 2 &middot; THE WAR IN THE WEST</div>
<h3 style="font-size: 20px; color: #0e1c42; margin: 18px 0 8px 0;">Two Fronts</h3><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">The land west of the Mississippi River was called the Trans-Mississippi <a href="#glossary" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="A large region where part of a war is fought.">theater</a>. It was far from Richmond and Washington, and it often had to fight on its own. Texas troops did much of that fighting.</p>
<div style="display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 12px; margin: 16px 0;">
  <div style="flex: 1; min-width: 240px; background: #EEF1F8; border-left: 5px solid #0E1C42; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 16px;">
    <div style="font-size: 15px; color: #0E1C42; margin-bottom: 6px;"><strong>The East</strong></div>
    <div style="font-size: 15px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.55;">Virginia, Maryland, Pennsylvania. Hood&rsquo;s Texas Brigade in Lee&rsquo;s army.</div>
  </div>
  <div style="flex: 1; min-width: 240px; background: #FDF3E3; border-left: 5px solid #C7922C; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 16px;">
    <div style="font-size: 15px; color: #0E1C42; margin-bottom: 6px;"><strong>The West of the Mississippi</strong></div>
    <div style="font-size: 15px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.55;">Louisiana, Arkansas, and Texas itself. The Trans-Mississippi <a href="#glossary" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="A large region where part of a war is fought.">theater</a>.</div>
  </div>
</div>
<p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">Texans also marched far to the west. In 1862, General Henry Sibley led about 2,500 Texans into New Mexico. He hoped to win the Southwest and a road to the gold of California. The Texans won a battle at Valverde, but they were turned back at Glorieta Pass and went home in defeat.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">A Texas division led by General John G. Walker became famous for long, fast marches in Louisiana and Arkansas. Soldiers called them &ldquo;Walker&rsquo;s Greyhounds.&rdquo; Texas <a href="#glossary" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="Soldiers who fight on horseback.">cavalry</a> fought there as well.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">In the spring of 1864, a Union army moved up the Red River in Louisiana. It aimed to take Shreveport, the Confederate headquarters west of the river, and to threaten Texas. At Mansfield, Louisiana, on April 8, Confederate forces, with many Texans among them, struck first and drove the Union army back.</p><div class="optima-widget ow-select" data-ra-skip>
  <div class="widget-kicker">&#9889; TRY IT &middot; EAST OR WEST?</div>
  <div class="widget-question">Texans fought on two very different fronts. For each battle, decide where the Texans were fighting.</div>
  <div class="widget-row" data-correct="1" data-fb="Right. Texans struck first at Mansfield, in the Trans-Mississippi, far from Lee." data-hint="Think about where the Red River campaign took place. Was it near Lee’s army?">
    <div class="widget-row-prompt">1. Mansfield, Louisiana, April 1864</div>
    <div class="widget-options"><button type="button" data-choice="0">Fought in the East with Lee’s army</button><button type="button" data-choice="1">Fought in the West or on Texas ground</button></div>
  </div>
  <div class="widget-row" data-correct="0" data-fb="Right. The 1st Texas lost more than 80 of every 100 men in a single day." data-hint="Maryland is far closer to Virginia than to Texas. Which army fought there?">
    <div class="widget-row-prompt">2. Antietam, Maryland, September 1862</div>
    <div class="widget-options"><button type="button" data-choice="0">Fought in the East with Lee’s army</button><button type="button" data-choice="1">Fought in the West or on Texas ground</button></div>
  </div>
  <div class="widget-row" data-correct="0" data-fb="Right. This is where the Texans shouted “Lee to the rear.”" data-hint="The Texas Brigade served in Lee’s army. Where did that army fight?">
    <div class="widget-row-prompt">3. The Wilderness, Virginia, May 1864</div>
    <div class="widget-options"><button type="button" data-choice="0">Fought in the East with Lee’s army</button><button type="button" data-choice="1">Fought in the West or on Texas ground</button></div>
  </div>
  <div class="widget-row" data-correct="1" data-fb="Right. Sibley’s Texans marched far to the west, and they were turned back." data-hint="Sibley marched his men west, away from Virginia.">
    <div class="widget-row-prompt">4. Glorieta Pass, New Mexico, 1862</div>
    <div class="widget-options"><button type="button" data-choice="0">Fought in the East with Lee’s army</button><button type="button" data-choice="1">Fought in the West or on Texas ground</button></div>
  </div>
  <div class="widget-row" data-correct="0" data-fb="Right. The Texans attacked on the second day, and Hood was badly wounded." data-hint="Pennsylvania is in the North. Which Confederate army went there?">
    <div class="widget-row-prompt">5. Gettysburg, Pennsylvania, July 1863</div>
    <div class="widget-options"><button type="button" data-choice="0">Fought in the East with Lee’s army</button><button type="button" data-choice="1">Fought in the West or on Texas ground</button></div>
  </div>
  <button class="widget-reset">Reset</button>
</div>
    </div>
    <div style="background: #FFFFFF; border-top: 3px solid #C7922C; border: 1px solid #D0D9E8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 20px 24px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <div style="font-size: 11px; color: #c7922c; margin-bottom: 14px;">&#128218; PART 3 &middot; THE WAR COMES HOME</div>
<h3 style="font-size: 20px; color: #0e1c42; margin: 18px 0 8px 0;">The War Reaches Texas</h3><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">Union ships <a href="#glossary" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="Using warships to keep ships from entering or leaving a port.">blockade</a>d the Gulf coast. In October 1862, they took Galveston. On New Year&rsquo;s Day 1863, Confederate General John Magruder struck back. His men fought from two steamboats lined with cotton bales and from the shore. They won Galveston back.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">In September 1863, a Union fleet tried to land an army at Sabine Pass, on the Louisiana border. Lieutenant Dick Dowling and about 40 men held a small fort with six cannons. They drove off the fleet and captured two gunboats. It was one of the most famous Texas victories of the war.</p><h3 style="font-size: 20px; color: #0e1c42; margin: 18px 0 8px 0;">The Cotton Road</h3><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">The blockade made it hard to sell cotton overseas. So Texans found another way. Cotton was hauled in wagons to Brownsville, then across the R&iacute;o Grande to the Mexican port of <a href="#foreign" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="mat-uh-MOR-ohs. A Mexican city on the south bank of the Río Grande."><em>Matamoros</em></a>. Mexico was a neutral country. Ships from other nations could dock there, take the cotton, and bring back guns and supplies.</p><div id="foreign" style="background: #E8F6FB; border-left: 5px solid #2196D0; border-top: 3px solid #55C8E8; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 20px; margin: 14px 0;"><div style="font-size: 11px; color: #1B6A94; margin-bottom: 8px;">&#128161; FOREIGN LANGUAGE VOCABULARY</div><div style="font-size: 16px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.55; margin-bottom: 6px;"><strong>Matamoros</strong><button type="button" class="gloss-audio" aria-label="Play pronunciation of Matamoros" onclick="speakGloss(event,'mat uh mor ohs')">&#128266;</button> <span style="color: #6b7a99;">(mat-uh-MOR-ohs)</span>: A Mexican city on the south bank of the Río Grande.</div><div style="font-size: 16px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.55; margin-bottom: 6px;"><strong>Tejano</strong><button type="button" class="gloss-audio" aria-label="Play pronunciation of Tejano" onclick="speakGloss(event,'teh hah noh')">&#128266;</button> <span style="color: #6b7a99;">(teh-HAH-noh)</span>: A Texan of Mexican descent.</div></div><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">A <a href="#foreign" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="teh-HAH-noh. A Texan of Mexican descent."><em>Tejano</em></a> named Santos Benavides, from Laredo, rose to the rank of colonel. His 33rd Texas <a href="#glossary" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="Soldiers who fight on horseback.">cavalry</a> guarded this route. In 1864, his men defended Laredo against a Union raid.</p><h3 style="font-size: 20px; color: #0e1c42; margin: 18px 0 8px 0;">The Border Again</h3><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">The cotton trade made the R&iacute;o Grande important once more, the same river that had caused the war with Mexico fifteen years earlier. Union ships could stop vessels at Confederate ports, but they could not easily stop neutral ships at a Mexican port. That is why the Texas border mattered so much to the Confederacy.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">Enslaved Texans did much of the work that kept the war effort going. They farmed, hauled cotton, and built defenses. Some risked everything to escape across the R&iacute;o Grande into Mexico, where slavery was against the law.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">Back home, women ran farms and ranches while the men were away. Prices climbed, and goods like cloth and salt were hard to find. The war reached almost every Texas family.</p><h3 style="font-size: 20px; color: #0e1c42; margin: 18px 0 8px 0;">Not Every Texan Agreed</h3><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">Not all Texans supported the Confederacy. Some, especially among German settlers in the Hill Country, were <a href="#glossary" style="border-bottom: 1.5px dotted #C7922C; cursor: help; color: #0E1C42; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;" title="A person who stayed loyal to the United States during the Civil War.">Unionist</a>s. In 1862, Confederate forces attacked a group of them near the Nueces River. In the same year, in Gainesville, dozens of men accused of disloyalty were hanged. Texas was divided, and some people paid a heavy price for their beliefs.</p><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">The Union tried to close the Cotton Road. In November 1863, Union soldiers landed near the mouth of the R&iacute;o Grande and took Brownsville. They held it for months, until Texas troops under Colonel John Ford retook the area in the summer of 1864.</p><h3 style="font-size: 20px; color: #0e1c42; margin: 18px 0 8px 0;">The Last Battle</h3><p style="font-size: 17px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">Robert E. Lee surrendered in Virginia in April 1865. More than a month later, in May, Texans fought a small battle at Palmito Ranch near Brownsville. The Confederates won. It was the last land battle of the war.</p><div class="optima-widget ow-seq" data-ra-skip data-next="0" data-done="The war reached Texas early and ended there late. The last shots of the war were fired on Texas ground." data-hint="Not yet. Which of these came earlier?">
  <div class="widget-kicker">&#9889; TRY IT &middot; THE WAR COMES TO TEXAS</div>
  <div class="widget-question">These six events happened on or near Texas ground. Click them in the order they happened.</div>
  <div class="seq-msg"></div>
  <div class="seq-pool"><button type="button" class="seq-item" data-n="2" data-date="September 1863" data-text="Dick Dowling holds Sabine Pass" data-fb="About 40 men with six cannons drove off a fleet.">Dick Dowling holds Sabine Pass</button><button type="button" class="seq-item" data-n="5" data-date="May 1865" data-text="The last land battle of the war, at Palmito Ranch" data-fb="The Confederates won, more than a month after Lee surrendered.">The last land battle of the war, at Palmito Ranch</button><button type="button" class="seq-item" data-n="0" data-date="October 1862" data-text="Union ships take Galveston" data-fb="The Confederates struck back soon after.">Union ships take Galveston</button><button type="button" class="seq-item" data-n="4" data-date="Summer 1864" data-text="Texas troops under Colonel Ford retake the area" data-fb="The trade through Matamoros went on.">Texas troops under Colonel Ford retake the area</button><button type="button" class="seq-item" data-n="1" data-date="January 1, 1863" data-text="Magruder wins Galveston back" data-fb="His men fought from two steamboats lined with cotton bales.">Magruder wins Galveston back</button><button type="button" class="seq-item" data-n="3" data-date="November 1863" data-text="Union soldiers take Brownsville" data-fb="The Cotton Road was in danger.">Union soldiers take Brownsville</button></div>
  <div class="seq-line"></div>
  <div class="widget-feedback"></div>
  <button class="widget-reset">Reset</button>
</div>
    </div>

    <div style="background: #FFFFFF; border-top: 3px solid #C7922C; border: 1px solid #D0D9E8; border-radius: 8px; padding: 20px 24px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <div style="font-size: 11px; color: #c7922c; margin-bottom: 12px;">&#128170; WHAT TO CARRY FORWARD</div>
        <p style="font-size: 18px; color: #1a2340; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0;">Texans fought on two fronts, in Virginia and at home, and they paid for it in lives and in loss. A good account of the war keeps three things in view at once: the courage of the soldiers, the cause their government served, and the Texans who disagreed with it.</p>
    </div>
</div>

